not sure what clang-tidy is seeing there, but there could be a possibility for the lifetime of the ISL69269 and with it, the I2C instance to end before the co-routine starts running.

The proposed fix is to have the phosphor::i2c::I2C i2cInterface be a shared ptr.

Ok. Here is the reason why this is not necessary:

In the original code the struct looks like this:
```
struct raa_data {
  union {
    uint8_t raw[32];
    struct {
      uint8_t addr;
      uint8_t cmd;
      uint8_t data[];
    } __attribute__((packed));
  };
  uint8_t len;  // [cmd] ~ data[N]
  uint8_t pec;
};
```
The parse function writes into `raw`:
```
...
memcpy(config->pdata[dcnt].raw, &buf[2], config->pdata[dcnt].len+1);
...
```
So because of the union we write into raw, but read from cmd-pointer into data. 
See the program function:
```
...
for (i = 0; i < config->wr_cnt; i++) {
    memcpy(tbuf, &config->pdata[i].cmd, config->pdata[i].len);
#ifdef VR_RAA_DEBUG
    printf("Write RAA[%d]:", i);
    for (int j = 0; j < config->pdata[i].len; j++) {
      printf(" %02X", tbuf[j]);
    }
    printf("\n");
#endif
    if ((ret = vr_xfer(bus, addr, tbuf, config->pdata[i].len, rbuf, 0))) {
      break;
    }
    printf("\rupdated: %d %%  ", ((i+1)*100)/config->wr_cnt);
    fflush(stdout);
  }
...
```
So, the memcopy takes the address from cmd and copies the amount of bytes from len field. 

The `__attribute__((packed))` makes sure, we safe some memory and the alignment for the other fields (len/pec) is not too far from the rest.

In my implementation the struct does not use an union:
```
struct Data
    {
        uint8_t len;
        uint8_t pec;
        uint8_t addr;
        uint8_t cmd;
        uint8_t data[30];
    };
```
and my parse function sets the fields seperatly:
```
                // According to documentation:
                // 00 05 C2 E7 08 00 F6
                //  |  |  |  |  |  |  |
                //  |  |  |  |  |  |  - Packet Error Code (CRC8)
                //  |  |  |  |  -  - Data
                //  |  |  |  - Command Code
                //  |  |  - Address
                //  |  - Size of data (including Addr, Cmd, CRC8)
                //  - Line type (0x00 - Data, 0x49 header information)
                configuration.pData[dcnt].len = sepLine[1] - 2;
                configuration.pData[dcnt].pec =
                    sepLine[3 + configuration.pData[dcnt].len];
                configuration.pData[dcnt].addr = sepLine[2];
                configuration.pData[dcnt].cmd = sepLine[3];
                std::memcpy(configuration.pData[dcnt].data, sepLine + 2,
                            configuration.pData[dcnt].len + 1);
```

as well as my program function writes the cmd and data seperatly:
```
        tbuf[0] = configuration.pData[i].cmd;
        std::memcpy(tbuf + 1, &configuration.pData[i].data,
                    configuration.pData[i].len - 1);
```

Because we dont have a union and data is a fixed size array, we dont run in the same problems in CPP as we do in C, so `__attribute__((packed))` is simply not required. The cant be a mismatch in my code, because there is no union.

